<p>Last week, <a href="https://leancrew.com/all-this/2025/12/tweaking-my-screenshot-macro/">my SnapClip macro</a> crapped out with an error message. In the process of fixing it, I learned something about AppleScript that probably won’t ever help me in the future.</p>
<p>SnapClip is a Keyboard Maestro macro that puts a graphical user interface on a Python script, <code>process-screenshot</code>. The problem that led to the error was a particular branch of <code>process-screenshot</code>: the branch that puts the screenshot onto the clipboard. I’m not going to rehash the design of SnapClip or <code>process-screenshot</code> here—you can read the <a href="https://leancrew.com/all-this/2025/12/tweaking-my-screenshot-macro/">old post</a> about them if you want the details.</p>
<p>The branch of <code>process-screenshot</code> that failed runs the <a href="https://www.alecjacobson.com/weblog/3816.html"><code>impbcopy</code></a> command-line utility written by Alec Jacobson over a decade ago. I’ve had <code>impbcopy</code> running successfully on my Macs for many years, but last week it failed with the error message</p>
<pre><code>Bad CPU type in executable
</code></pre>
<p>I assumed the problem was that <code>impbcopy</code> had been compiled back when I was using an Intel Mac, and it had continued to work because of <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102527">Rosetta</a>. I thought that because I had Rosetta installed before, it would be updated along with macOS 27, but it wasn’t. Running</p>
<pre><code>softwareupdate --install-rosetta
</code></pre>
<p>as described on the <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102527">Rosetta page</a> got SnapClip working again.</p>
<p>But macOS 27 is the end of the road for Rosetta, so it seemed like a change was due. I installed the <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/installing-the-command-line-tools">Xcode Command Line Tools</a> and tried to recompile <code>impbcopy</code> for Apple Silicon, but that failed with error messages that I couldn’t interpret because I’m not an Objective-C programmer. So I had four options:</p>
<ol>
<li>Learn enough about <code>gcc</code>/Objective-C/<code>clang</code> to get the compilation to work. Even if I could do this, I assumed it would be a lot of work with no future value for me.</li>
<li>See if I could rewrite the SnapClip macro to use Keyboard Maestro features to put the image on the clipboard. This was more appealing, but I really liked the way the macro was structured and preferred to leave it as a GUI wrapper.</li>
<li>Look into using Shortcuts to put the image on the clipboard and—if it can—replace the call to <code>impbcopy</code> with an equivalent call to the <a href="https://leancrew.com/all-this/man/man1/shortcuts.html"><code>shortcuts</code> utility</a>. But I <em>hate</em> Shortcuts and would rather avoid it if possible.</li>
<li>Work out an AppleScript solution and put a call to <a href="https://leancrew.com/all-this/man/man1/osascript.html"><code>osascript</code></a> into <code>process-screenshot</code>. I knew that AppleScript has commands for reading files and putting things on the clipboard, so I went with this, even though AppleScript may be more of a dead end than Objective-C.</li>
</ol>
<p>Apple’s online documentation for AppleScript sucks, but I have three old AppleScript books that collectively weigh over nine pounds:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://lccn.loc.gov/2006274517"><em>AppleScript: The Definitive Guide</em></a> by Matt Neuburg.</li>
<li><a href="https://lccn.loc.gov/2009278244"><em>AppleScript 1-2-3</em></a> by Sal Soghoian and Bill Cheeseman.</li>
<li><a href="https://lccn.loc.gov/2010485146"><em>Learn AppleScript</em></a> by Hamish Sanderson and Hanaan Rosenthal.</li>
</ul>
<p>The answer (more or less) was in <em>Learn AppleScript</em> and led to just a couple of changes to <code>process-screenshot</code>. Here’s the new version:</p>
<pre><code>python:
 1:  #!/usr/bin/env python3
 2:  
 3:  import tempfile
 4:  from PIL import Image
 5:  import os
 6:  import subprocess
 7:  import shutil
 8:  from datetime import datetime
 9:  import urllib.parse
10:  import argparse
11:  
12:  # Handle the arguments
13:  desc = 'Process a screenshot saved to a file.'
14:  ep = '''If a title is given, it saves the image to a file on the
15:  Desktop with a filename of the form yyyymmdd-title.png.
16:  If no title is given, the image is put on the clipboard and the
17:  upload option is ignored.'''
18:  parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=desc, epilog=ep,
19:            formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
20:  parser.add_argument('-b', '--background', help='add desktop background border', action='store_true')
21:  parser.add_argument('-u', '--upload', help='upload to images directory and print URL', action='store_true')
22:  parser.add_argument('-t', '--title', help='image title', type=str)
23:  parser.add_argument('ssfile', help='path to screenshot file')
24:  args = parser.parse_args()
25:  
26:  # Parameters
27:  type = "png"
28:  bgcolor = (85, 111, 137)
29:  border = 32
30:  optimizer = '/Applications/ImageOptim.app/Contents/MacOS/ImageOptim'
31:  ascript = f'set the clipboard to (read POSIX file "{args.ssfile}" as picture)'
32:  
33:  # Add a desktop background border if asked for
34:  if args.background:
35:    snap = Image.open(args.ssfile)
36:    # Make a solid-colored background bigger than the screenshot.
37:    snapsize = tuple([ x + 2*border for x in snap.size ])
38:    bg = Image.new('RGBA', snapsize, bgcolor)
39:    bg.alpha_composite(snap, dest=(border, border))
40:    bg.save(args.ssfile)
41:  
42:  # Optimize the file
43:  subprocess.run([optimizer, args.ssfile], stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
44:  
45:  # Save it to a Desktop file if a title was given; otherwise,
46:  # save it to the clipboard
47:  if args.title:
48:    sdate = datetime.now().strftime("%Y%m%d")
49:    desktop = os.environ['HOME'] + "/Desktop/"
50:    fname = f'{desktop}{sdate}-{args.title}.{type}'
51:    shutil.copyfile(args.ssfile, fname)
52:    bname = os.path.basename(fname)
53:  
54:    # Upload the file and print the URL if asked
55:    if args.upload:
56:      year = datetime.now().strftime("%Y")
57:      server = f'user@server.com:path/to/images{year}/'
58:      port = '123456789'
59:      subprocess.run(['scp', '-P', port, fname, server])
60:      bname = urllib.parse.quote(bname)
61:      print(f'https://leancrew.com/all-this/images{year}/{bname}')
62:  else:
63:    subprocess.run(['osascript', '-e', ascript])
64:  
65:  # Delete the temporary file
66:  os.remove(args.ssfile)
</code></pre>
<p>The changes are:</p>
<ul>
<li>Line 31, which is an added line that defines the AppleScript command that does what <code>impbcopy</code> used to do; and</li>
<li>Line 63, which runs the <code>ascript</code> command via <code>osascript</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The AppleScript that does the work is just one line:</p>
<pre><code>set the clipboard to (read POSIX file "&lt;name of file&gt;" as picture)
</code></pre>
<p>Very simple, and I suspect it will work for quite a while. I feel certain that Apple would like to kill AppleScript, but I don’t think it can—too many valuable customers (a group that doesn’t include me) would get very angry at having their longstanding automations die.</p>
<p>One thing that kind of surprised me was the <code>as picture</code> part of the command. <em>Learn AppleScript</em> says that you typically have to include an image type in the command: <code>as GIF picture</code> or <code>as JPEG picture</code> or <code>as TIFF picture</code>. The book suggests that a bare <code>as picture</code> is supposed to work only on <code>PICT</code> images. Never heard of a <code>PICT</code> image? That’s probably because it was the native Mac image format back in the classic days. It was one of Bill Atkinson’s contributions to the Macintosh Toolbox. Nobody saves image files as <code>PICT</code>s anymore.</p>
<p>And yet the unadorned <code>as picture</code> works. I created this test image in Acorn,</p>
<p><img alt="Test image" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261106-Test%20image.png" title="Test image" width="80%"/></p>
<p>and saved it in several formats: <code>BMP</code>, <code>GIF</code>, <code>HEIC</code>, <code>JPEG</code>, <code>JPEG 2000</code>, <code>PNG</code>, and <code>TIFF</code>. I put the files in a folder named <code>clipboard-test</code> on my Desktop,</p>
<p><img alt="Folder of images" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261106-Folder%20of%20images.png" title="Folder of images" width="100%"/></p>
<p>and ran this AppleScript for each of the different file extensions:</p>
<pre><code>applescript:
set filePath to path to desktop folder as text
set imageFile to (filePath &amp; "clipboard-test:Test image.bmp") as alias
set the clipboard to (read file imageFile as picture)
</code></pre>
<p>Each time I ran the script, the clipboard was loaded with the image, no matter which file was being read. I didn’t have to specify the format.</p>
<p>I suspect that if I were using AppleScript to write image data to a file, it wouldn’t be as forgiving. But <code>process-screenshot</code> uses Python and <a href="https://pypi.org/project/pillow/">Pillow</a> to save image files, so I don’t need to worry about AppleScript’s idiosyncrasies for that aspect of the script.</p>
